1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,374
Welcome back to my study series on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:05,374 --> 00:00:10,290
In chapter one we learned that a language model predicts the next word.

3
00:00:10,290 --> 00:00:13,060
But a computer only understands numbers.

4
00:00:13,060 --> 00:00:23,308
So in this chapter we answer a deceptively simple question: how do you turn a word into numbers in a way that actually captures what the word means?

5
00:00:23,308 --> 00:00:28,779
The answer is embeddings, and it is one of the most elegant ideas in all of AI.

6
00:00:28,779 --> 00:00:38,750
I've also run real experiments with real word vectors for this video, so you'll see the ideas working, and occasionally failing, on actual data.

7
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:40,985
A quick orientation.

8
00:00:40,985 --> 00:00:48,184
Chapter one gave us the big picture: a model is a big pile of numbers trained to predict the next token.

9
00:00:48,184 --> 00:00:53,999
Today we zoom in on the very first step of that machine, turning words into numbers.

10
00:00:53,999 --> 00:00:59,744
Chapter three adds attention, and chapter four puts the whole transformer together.

11
00:00:59,744 --> 00:01:04,798
These four chapters are conceptual, so the book has no notebook for them.

12
00:01:04,798 --> 00:01:13,727
The small code snippets you'll see today are my own illustrations, clearly labelled, run with the open source GloVe word vectors.

13
00:01:14,578 --> 00:01:15,648
Here's the plan.

14
00:01:15,648 --> 00:01:21,668
We start with two naive ways of turning words into numbers, and see exactly why they fail.

15
00:01:21,668 --> 00:01:29,026
Then we introduce the key idea: give each word a position in a space, so distance means difference in meaning.

16
00:01:29,026 --> 00:01:38,925
We'll see why that space needs hundreds of dimensions, and then look at the famous king minus man plus woman equals queen result, with real numbers.

17
00:01:38,925 --> 00:01:45,079
After that, we'll see where embeddings come from: context, learned without any human labels.

18
00:01:45,079 --> 00:01:56,918
We'll separate static from contextual embeddings, see why simply averaging word vectors loses meaning, and finish with exercises, the checkpoint, takeaways and review questions.

19
00:01:57,768 --> 00:01:59,740
Let's set up the vocabulary.

20
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:05,514
A vector is just a list of numbers, which you can think of as a position in space.

21
00:02:05,514 --> 00:02:08,190
A dimension is one axis of that space.

22
00:02:08,190 --> 00:02:14,105
An embedding is a word's vector, arranged so that its position reflects its meaning.

23
00:02:14,105 --> 00:02:18,119
The space where all those vectors live is the word space.

24
00:02:18,119 --> 00:02:24,668
The distributional hypothesis says that words used in similar contexts have similar meanings.

25
00:02:24,668 --> 00:02:31,216
Cosine similarity measures how similar two vectors are by their angle, from minus one to one.

26
00:02:31,216 --> 00:02:35,723
Word2Vec is the 2013 system that made learned embeddings famous.

27
00:02:35,723 --> 00:02:43,117
And self-supervised learning means the training signal comes from the data itself, not from human labels.

28
00:02:43,967 --> 00:02:44,549
Part one.

29
00:02:44,549 --> 00:02:46,036
The computer's dilemma.

30
00:02:46,886 --> 00:02:51,800
Imagine explaining the idea of a cat to a friend who only understands numbers.

31
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:55,202
That is exactly the position we're in with a computer.

32
00:02:55,202 --> 00:02:56,903
It can't read the word cat.

33
00:02:56,903 --> 00:02:59,485
It can only store and manipulate numbers.

34
00:02:59,485 --> 00:03:02,383
So we need some mapping from words to numbers.

35
00:03:02,383 --> 00:03:03,958
Making a mapping is easy.

36
00:03:03,958 --> 00:03:11,203
The hard part is making one where the numbers themselves carry meaning, so that the model can compute with meaning.

37
00:03:11,203 --> 00:03:13,659
Let's try the obvious approaches first.

38
00:03:14,509 --> 00:03:19,306
The first instinct is to use the codes computers already use for text.

39
00:03:19,306 --> 00:03:28,145
In ASCII, c is ninety nine, a is ninety seven and t is one hundred and sixteen, so cat is ninety nine, ninety seven, one sixteen.

40
00:03:28,145 --> 00:03:31,366
Car is ninety nine, ninety seven, one fourteen.

41
00:03:31,366 --> 00:03:36,231
Those are almost identical, but a cat and a car have nothing in common.

42
00:03:36,231 --> 00:03:45,002
Meanwhile kitten and feline, which mean essentially the same creature, get completely different numbers, even different lengths.

43
00:03:45,002 --> 00:03:51,922
In the chart you can see cat and car almost on top of each other, while kitten and feline wander off.

44
00:03:52,772 --> 00:03:58,934
The core problem is that ASCII captures spelling, and spelling is mostly an accident of history.

45
00:03:58,934 --> 00:04:02,207
There's nothing cat-like about the letters c, a, t.

46
00:04:02,207 --> 00:04:10,871
Words that look alike, like cat, car, cart and cut, have unrelated meanings, and words that mean the same thing can look nothing alike.

47
00:04:10,871 --> 00:04:15,492
So whatever numbers we pick, they have to reflect meaning, not spelling.

48
00:04:16,342 --> 00:04:17,461
Second attempt.

49
00:04:17,461 --> 00:04:21,491
Forget letters, and give every word its own ID number.

50
00:04:21,491 --> 00:04:27,460
Cat is one, dog is two, fish is three, feline four, kitten five, automobile six.

51
00:04:27,460 --> 00:04:30,519
Now cat and car are definitely different.

52
00:04:30,519 --> 00:04:31,638
Problem solved?

53
00:04:32,488 --> 00:04:33,266
Not really.

54
00:04:33,266 --> 00:04:34,680
IDs are just labels.

55
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:37,367
The gap between two IDs means nothing.

56
00:04:37,367 --> 00:04:46,488
Cat is one and kitten is five, while automobile is six, so by these numbers a kitten is almost as unrelated to a cat as a car is.

57
00:04:46,488 --> 00:04:51,367
And arithmetic is nonsense: cat plus dog equals three, which is fish.

58
00:04:51,367 --> 00:04:53,842
So we can write down our wish list.

59
00:04:53,842 --> 00:04:56,741
Similar words should get similar numbers.

60
00:04:56,741 --> 00:04:59,923
Unrelated words should get different numbers.

61
00:04:59,923 --> 00:05:03,600
And relationships between words should be preserved.

62
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:09,752
It turns out we can have all three, but only if we stop using a single number per word.

63
00:05:10,602 --> 00:05:11,349
Part two.

64
00:05:11,349 --> 00:05:12,927
Words as locations.

65
00:05:13,777 --> 00:05:15,313
Here's the key insight.

66
00:05:15,313 --> 00:05:19,253
Instead of giving each word one number, give it a position.

67
00:05:19,253 --> 00:05:20,589
Think of a city map.

68
00:05:20,589 --> 00:05:22,459
Every place has coordinates.

69
00:05:22,459 --> 00:05:31,007
Two cafés next to each other have similar coordinates, and a café and a park on opposite sides of town have very different ones.

70
00:05:31,007 --> 00:05:36,817
What if words had coordinates too, arranged so that nearby words have similar meanings?

71
00:05:37,667 --> 00:05:41,654
Here's a toy two dimensional word space that I've drawn.

72
00:05:41,654 --> 00:05:48,347
The horizontal axis is a made up gender axis, masculine on the left and feminine on the right.

73
00:05:48,347 --> 00:05:52,976
The vertical axis is age, young at the bottom and old at the top.

74
00:05:52,976 --> 00:05:58,458
Boy sits at one comma two, man at one comma seven, woman at nine comma seven.

75
00:05:58,458 --> 00:06:04,368
Boy and girl share a height because they're the same age, and differ left to right.

76
00:06:04,368 --> 00:06:06,932
Man and woman show the same pattern.

77
00:06:06,932 --> 00:06:17,327
Notice the distances: boy to man is exactly five, boy to woman is the square root of eight squared plus five squared, about nine point four three.

78
00:06:17,327 --> 00:06:22,098
So boy is closer to man than to woman, which matches our intuition.

79
00:06:22,948 --> 00:06:27,092
In this picture each word is a vector, a list of two numbers.

80
00:06:27,092 --> 00:06:35,175
The straight line distance between two vectors is the square root of the sum of squared differences in each coordinate.

81
00:06:35,175 --> 00:06:39,590
Boy to man differs only in age, by five, so the distance is five.

82
00:06:39,590 --> 00:06:48,013
Boy to woman differs by eight in gender and five in age, giving the square root of eighty nine, about nine point four three.

83
00:06:48,013 --> 00:06:54,263
One important warning that the book also makes: these neat named axes are just for teaching.

84
00:06:54,263 --> 00:07:03,433
In real embeddings the dimensions are learned automatically, and they almost never line up with tidy human concepts like gender or age.

85
00:07:04,283 --> 00:07:13,390
In high dimensional embeddings the standard measure is not distance but cosine similarity, which looks at the angle between two vectors.

86
00:07:13,390 --> 00:07:20,086
You take the dot product, multiply matching coordinates and add them up, and divide by both lengths.

87
00:07:20,086 --> 00:07:24,305
In my example, a is four comma three and b is three comma four.

88
00:07:24,305 --> 00:07:34,149
Their dot product is twelve plus twelve, twenty four, and each has length five, so the cosine is twenty four over twenty five, zero point nine six.

89
00:07:34,149 --> 00:07:35,756
Very similar directions.

90
00:07:35,756 --> 00:07:41,515
Vector c points elsewhere, and its cosine with a is about minus zero point five seven.

91
00:07:41,515 --> 00:07:47,274
Cosine runs from one, same direction, through zero, unrelated, to minus one, opposite.

92
00:07:47,274 --> 00:07:52,831
Because it ignores length, it compares what a vector points at, not how long it is.

93
00:07:53,682 --> 00:07:56,296
Two dimensions quickly run out of room.

94
00:07:56,296 --> 00:07:59,917
A king is similar to a queen because both are royalty.

95
00:07:59,917 --> 00:08:03,202
A king is similar to a man because both are male.

96
00:08:03,202 --> 00:08:07,359
And a king is similar to a president because both are leaders.

97
00:08:07,359 --> 00:08:14,332
Each of those is a different kind of similarity, and to represent all of them at once we need more axes.

98
00:08:15,182 --> 00:08:21,525
Here's the same idea in three dimensions, again with illustrative axes: gender, age, and now royalty.

99
00:08:21,525 --> 00:08:26,423
The commoners, boy, girl, man and woman, sit on the floor of the cube in blue.

100
00:08:26,423 --> 00:08:30,881
The royals, prince, princess, king and queen, float above them in gold.

101
00:08:30,881 --> 00:08:36,345
Prince is now closer to king than to boy, and princess is closer to queen than to girl.

102
00:08:36,345 --> 00:08:38,731
And that's with just three dimensions.

103
00:08:38,731 --> 00:08:44,132
We can't draw seven hundred and sixty eight, but the maths works exactly the same way.

104
00:08:44,982 --> 00:08:45,719
Part three.

105
00:08:45,719 --> 00:08:47,328
The magic of embeddings.

106
00:08:48,178 --> 00:08:54,002
This list of numbers that places a word in a high dimensional space is called an embedding.

107
00:08:54,002 --> 00:09:01,938
The book shows a stylised example: king and queen have similar numbers in each position, and banana has very different ones.

108
00:09:01,938 --> 00:09:03,922
Those numbers are illustrative.

109
00:09:03,922 --> 00:09:14,930
On the next slides I'll show you the real thing, using GloVe, a set of one hundred dimensional word vectors trained on Wikipedia and newswire text that anyone can download.

110
00:09:15,780 --> 00:09:17,955
Here's the tiny program I used.

111
00:09:17,955 --> 00:09:26,374
It loads the GloVe vectors through the gensim library, about four hundred thousand words, each with one hundred numbers.

112
00:09:26,374 --> 00:09:35,004
Then it prints part of a vector, computes a cosine similarity, and asks for the nearest words to king minus man plus woman.

113
00:09:35,004 --> 00:09:38,863
This is my own snippet, not from the book's repository.

114
00:09:39,713 --> 00:09:42,798
And here are the real cosine similarities.

115
00:09:42,798 --> 00:09:45,443
Cat and dog: zero point eight eight.

116
00:09:45,443 --> 00:09:48,234
King and queen: zero point seven five.

117
00:09:48,234 --> 00:10:01,162
Cat and kitten: zero point five six, and cat and feline: zero point four nine, lower than you might expect, because feline appears in more formal, scientific contexts than cat.

118
00:10:01,162 --> 00:10:06,745
Cat and car, despite the near identical spelling: only zero point three one.

119
00:10:06,745 --> 00:10:09,684
And king and banana: zero point one six.

120
00:10:09,684 --> 00:10:12,548
The ordering is broadly what we wanted.

121
00:10:12,548 --> 00:10:20,262
Notice that these vectors capture how words are used, which is not always the same as dictionary meaning.

122
00:10:21,112 --> 00:10:22,670
Why so many dimensions?

123
00:10:22,670 --> 00:10:24,837
Think about describing a person.

124
00:10:24,837 --> 00:10:27,953
With just height you can tell tall from short.

125
00:10:27,953 --> 00:10:32,153
Add weight and you can tell tall and thin from tall and heavy.

126
00:10:32,153 --> 00:10:36,895
Add age, hair and eye colour, and descriptions get much more specific.

127
00:10:36,895 --> 00:10:38,182
Words are the same.

128
00:10:38,182 --> 00:10:53,626
Two dimensions give crude groupings, around a hundred can separate mammals from reptiles and pets from wild animals, and seven hundred and sixty eight can capture the difference between a leisurely stroll and a deliberate march.

129
00:10:53,626 --> 00:11:03,923
Seven sixty eight is common because GPT-2 small and BERT base used it, and very large models go to twelve thousand two hundred and eighty eight or more.

130
00:11:03,923 --> 00:11:07,852
Importantly, no single dimension means something nameable.

131
00:11:07,852 --> 00:11:10,358
Meaning is spread across all of them.

132
00:11:11,208 --> 00:11:12,621
Why the name embedding?

133
00:11:12,621 --> 00:11:19,011
In mathematics, an embedding maps objects from one space into another while keeping important structure.

134
00:11:19,011 --> 00:11:28,227
Here we take discrete symbols, where cat and car have no inherent relationship, and place them into a continuous space where position encodes meaning.

135
00:11:28,227 --> 00:11:36,213
Once they're in that space, we can do all the things computers are good at: measure distances, find neighbours, and do arithmetic.

136
00:11:36,213 --> 00:11:39,347
That's what makes language processing mathematical.

137
00:11:40,197 --> 00:11:42,488
Here's a nice fact from the book.

138
00:11:42,488 --> 00:11:52,555
In models trained on many languages, the English word dog and the German word Hund end up as neighbours, because they appear in similar contexts.

139
00:11:52,555 --> 00:11:55,610
Nobody tells the model they're translations.

140
00:11:55,610 --> 00:12:05,747
I tested this with GloVe, which is trained on English only, and the cosine between dog and hund is basically zero, minus zero point zero zero six.

141
00:12:05,747 --> 00:12:08,454
That's not a contradiction of the book.

142
00:12:08,454 --> 00:12:14,633
It's a useful lesson: the cross language alignment comes from multilingual training data.

143
00:12:14,633 --> 00:12:18,591
An embedding only knows what its training text showed it.

144
00:12:19,441 --> 00:12:20,166
Part four.

145
00:12:20,166 --> 00:12:21,617
The famous equation.

146
00:12:22,467 --> 00:12:28,026
In 2013, researchers at Google found that you could do arithmetic with word vectors.

147
00:12:28,026 --> 00:12:33,586
Take the vector for king, subtract the vector for man, and add the vector for woman.

148
00:12:33,586 --> 00:12:38,020
The result is a point in space that doesn't exactly match any word.

149
00:12:38,020 --> 00:12:45,102
But when you look for the nearest word in the vocabulary, leaving out the three input words, you get queen.

150
00:12:45,952 --> 00:12:52,877
Here's what that looks like with real GloVe vectors, projected down to two dimensions so we can see it.

151
00:12:52,877 --> 00:12:59,332
The arrow from man to woman, and the arrow from king to the computed point, are the same vector.

152
00:12:59,332 --> 00:13:03,501
The computed point, the gold cross, lands right next to queen.

153
00:13:03,501 --> 00:13:07,871
Its cosine similarity with queen is about zero point seven eight.

154
00:13:07,871 --> 00:13:14,259
The top three results from gensim are queen at zero point seven seven, then monarch and throne.

155
00:13:14,259 --> 00:13:23,471
Remember that a two dimensional projection is a squashed view of a hundred dimensions, so distances in this picture are only approximate.

156
00:13:24,321 --> 00:13:26,852
This chart shows why the trick works.

157
00:13:26,852 --> 00:13:31,434
I took ten real GloVe vectors and projected them to two dimensions.

158
00:13:31,434 --> 00:13:38,547
The gold arrows go from man to woman, king to queen, boy to girl, uncle to aunt, and prince to princess.

159
00:13:38,547 --> 00:13:41,762
They're roughly parallel and of similar length.

160
00:13:41,762 --> 00:13:48,876
The embedding has learned something like a consistent gender direction, without anybody defining gender.

161
00:13:49,726 --> 00:13:53,857
So embeddings do more than put similar words near each other.

162
00:13:53,857 --> 00:14:00,156
They organise words so relationships are consistent: the same offset means the same relation.

163
00:14:00,156 --> 00:14:04,965
There's a gender direction, man to woman, king to queen, uncle to aunt.

164
00:14:04,965 --> 00:14:09,774
There's a capital of direction, from France to Paris and Italy to Rome.

165
00:14:09,774 --> 00:14:11,806
Nobody programmed any of this.

166
00:14:11,806 --> 00:14:14,515
It emerged purely from patterns in text.

167
00:14:15,365 --> 00:14:23,714
The book lists several analogy examples, and it's honest that these only work around forty to seventy percent of the time.

168
00:14:23,714 --> 00:14:24,672
So I ran them.

169
00:14:24,672 --> 00:14:27,477
Paris minus France plus Italy gives Rome.

170
00:14:27,477 --> 00:14:30,488
Walking minus walk plus swim gives swimming.

171
00:14:30,488 --> 00:14:33,089
Uncle minus man plus woman gives aunt.

172
00:14:33,089 --> 00:14:35,347
But three missed at the top spot.

173
00:14:35,347 --> 00:14:40,890
Tokyo minus Japan plus Germany gives Frankfurt first, with Berlin a close second.

174
00:14:40,890 --> 00:14:45,269
Bigger minus big plus small gives larger, just ahead of smaller.

175
00:14:45,269 --> 00:14:50,060
And brother minus man plus woman gives daughter, then wife and mother.

176
00:14:50,060 --> 00:14:52,113
Sister isn't in the top three.

177
00:14:52,113 --> 00:14:57,108
Four out of seven, which fits the book's forty to seventy percent nicely.

178
00:14:57,958 --> 00:14:59,109
Why do they miss?

179
00:14:59,109 --> 00:15:07,161
Often the right offset lands near the starting word's own neighbourhood, so bigger plus a little nudge lands on larger.

180
00:15:07,161 --> 00:15:16,026
Words that are frequent in similar contexts compete: in newswire text, Frankfurt shows up constantly as Germany's financial centre.

181
00:15:16,026 --> 00:15:19,477
Different training data produces different answers.

182
00:15:19,477 --> 00:15:26,515
And training data carries biases, which show up as directions in the space too, which is a real concern.

183
00:15:26,515 --> 00:15:28,545
The arithmetic is approximate.

184
00:15:28,545 --> 00:15:33,756
But the fact that it works at all shows embeddings capture genuine structure.

185
00:15:33,756 --> 00:15:36,395
They are a map of meaning, a fuzzy one.

186
00:15:37,245 --> 00:15:40,744
This connects to chapter one's lossy compression idea.

187
00:15:40,744 --> 00:15:50,268
Embeddings are the first stage of compressing the endless variety of language into fixed size vectors, and the rest of the model keeps compressing.

188
00:15:50,268 --> 00:15:59,922
That's why a model can reason about combinations it's never seen exactly, and it's also why it sometimes fills gaps with plausible but wrong details.

189
00:16:00,772 --> 00:16:01,441
Part five.

190
00:16:01,441 --> 00:16:03,247
How are embeddings learned?

191
00:16:04,097 --> 00:16:06,388
Where do these numbers come from?

192
00:16:06,388 --> 00:16:09,303
The answer is beautifully simple: context.

193
00:16:09,303 --> 00:16:10,345
The linguist J.

194
00:16:10,345 --> 00:16:10,484
R.

195
00:16:10,484 --> 00:16:14,441
Firth said you shall know a word by the company it keeps.

196
00:16:14,441 --> 00:16:19,439
Fill in the blank: the blank sat on the mat, the blank chased the mouse.

197
00:16:19,439 --> 00:16:21,799
Cat, dog, kitten or puppy all fit.

198
00:16:21,799 --> 00:16:25,409
These words share contexts, so they must be related.

199
00:16:25,409 --> 00:16:29,852
Now: I deposited money at the blank, the blank approved my loan.

200
00:16:29,852 --> 00:16:32,074
That's bank, the financial kind.

201
00:16:32,074 --> 00:16:34,712
Different contexts, different cluster.

202
00:16:34,712 --> 00:16:37,349
This is the distributional hypothesis.

203
00:16:38,200 --> 00:16:40,896
Here's how Word2Vec style systems learn.

204
00:16:40,896 --> 00:16:42,716
Take a huge amount of text.

205
00:16:42,716 --> 00:16:44,806
Slide a small window across it.

206
00:16:44,806 --> 00:16:58,087
For each position, either predict the middle word from the words around it, which is called continuous bag of words, or predict the surrounding words from the middle one, which is called skip gram.

207
00:16:58,087 --> 00:17:04,154
Every time the prediction is wrong, nudge the vectors a little so the prediction improves.

208
00:17:04,154 --> 00:17:11,772
After billions of these tiny updates, words that keep showing up in similar contexts end up with similar vectors.

209
00:17:12,622 --> 00:17:13,670
What comes out?

210
00:17:13,670 --> 00:17:20,236
Cat and dog share so many contexts that GloVe puts them at a cosine of zero point eight eight.

211
00:17:20,236 --> 00:17:26,662
King appears near royalty, rulers and male words, so its vector encodes all of that at once.

212
00:17:26,662 --> 00:17:32,739
And bank appears both near river and near money, so it gets a single compromise vector.

213
00:17:32,739 --> 00:17:41,680
In GloVe, which is trained largely on news text, bank's nearest neighbours are banks, banking, credit, investment and financial.

214
00:17:41,680 --> 00:17:46,430
The money sense completely dominates, and the river sense is buried.

215
00:17:47,279 --> 00:17:51,088
What's remarkable is that none of this needed human labels.

216
00:17:51,088 --> 00:17:53,670
Nobody marked cat and kitten as related.

217
00:17:53,670 --> 00:17:57,866
The thing to predict is simply a word that's already in the text.

218
00:17:57,866 --> 00:18:03,030
That's self-supervised learning, and it's why we can train on billions of words.

219
00:18:03,030 --> 00:18:06,129
Labelling that much by hand would be impossible.

220
00:18:06,129 --> 00:18:12,197
And it's exactly the same principle that trains large language models: predict the next token.

221
00:18:13,047 --> 00:18:15,050
Now an important distinction.

222
00:18:15,050 --> 00:18:21,337
Word2Vec and GloVe give static embeddings: one fixed vector per word, whatever the context.

223
00:18:21,337 --> 00:18:25,689
So bank gets the same vector in river bank and in bank account.

224
00:18:25,689 --> 00:18:29,350
Modern language models produce contextual embeddings.

225
00:18:29,350 --> 00:18:43,028
They start from a lookup table too, but then the attention layers modify each vector based on the surrounding words, so bank in river bank ends up somewhere very different from bank in bank account.

226
00:18:43,028 --> 00:18:45,860
Static embeddings are the stepping stone.

227
00:18:45,860 --> 00:18:52,699
Contextual ones are what LLMs actually use, and attention, our next chapter, is how they get there.

228
00:18:53,549 --> 00:18:56,639
Here's a striking example from my GloVe run.

229
00:18:56,639 --> 00:19:03,661
The nearest neighbours of apple are Microsoft, IBM, Intel, software, Dell, PC, Macintosh and iPhone.

230
00:19:03,661 --> 00:19:06,189
Not a single fruit in the top eight.

231
00:19:06,189 --> 00:19:14,123
In news text, the company is mentioned far more than the fruit, and a single static vector can only lean one way.

232
00:19:14,123 --> 00:19:19,811
A contextual model would treat apple pie and Apple shares completely differently.

233
00:19:20,661 --> 00:19:21,382
Part six.

234
00:19:21,382 --> 00:19:23,306
From words to sentences.

235
00:19:24,156 --> 00:19:26,536
We can now represent single words.

236
00:19:26,536 --> 00:19:29,755
But language comes in sentences and documents.

237
00:19:29,755 --> 00:19:35,004
The simplest idea is to average the vectors of all the words in a sentence.

238
00:19:35,004 --> 00:19:44,102
That gives one fixed size vector for any sentence, and for some tasks, like finding similar documents, it works surprisingly well.

239
00:19:44,102 --> 00:19:45,852
But there's a fatal flaw.

240
00:19:46,702 --> 00:19:48,967
Dog bites man, and man bites dog.

241
00:19:48,967 --> 00:19:52,123
Same three words, so exactly the same average.

242
00:19:52,123 --> 00:19:57,613
I checked with the real vectors, and the two averages are identical, cosine one.

243
00:19:57,613 --> 00:20:01,867
But one is a boring Tuesday, and the other is front page news.

244
00:20:01,867 --> 00:20:06,465
Or take the movie was not good, versus the movie was good, not bad.

245
00:20:06,465 --> 00:20:09,621
Very similar bags of words, opposite meanings.

246
00:20:09,621 --> 00:20:12,366
Averaging just can't see the difference.

247
00:20:13,216 --> 00:20:15,858
Let's list what averaging throws away.

248
00:20:15,858 --> 00:20:16,622
Word order.

249
00:20:16,622 --> 00:20:21,975
Long range relationships, like linking was back to cat across a whole clause.

250
00:20:21,975 --> 00:20:25,381
Modifiers, like not flipping the meaning of good.

251
00:20:25,381 --> 00:20:28,439
And disambiguation, like which bank we mean.

252
00:20:28,439 --> 00:20:33,931
All of that depends on how words relate to each other, and averaging erases it.

253
00:20:34,781 --> 00:20:36,261
So here's what we need.

254
00:20:36,261 --> 00:20:39,480
Process words in sequence and keep track of order.

255
00:20:39,480 --> 00:20:42,119
Let words influence each other's meaning.

256
00:20:42,119 --> 00:20:44,887
Handle relationships across long distances.

257
00:20:44,887 --> 00:20:49,457
And give the same word different representations in different contexts.

258
00:20:49,457 --> 00:20:54,220
That is exactly what the attention mechanism does, and it's chapter three.

259
00:20:54,220 --> 00:21:00,914
Static embeddings are necessary, they give us a mathematical starting point, but they aren't sufficient.

260
00:21:01,764 --> 00:21:02,554
Part seven.

261
00:21:02,554 --> 00:21:03,919
Let's get hands on.

262
00:21:04,769 --> 00:21:07,280
You can explore embeddings yourself.

263
00:21:07,280 --> 00:21:14,951
The TensorFlow Embedding Projector at projector dot tensorflow dot org shows word vectors in three dimensions.

264
00:21:14,951 --> 00:21:19,136
Search for a word, see its neighbours, and rotate the cloud.

265
00:21:19,136 --> 00:21:22,553
There are also many word arithmetic demos online.

266
00:21:22,553 --> 00:21:28,342
Or run my five line gensim snippet from the notes, on your own machine or in Colab.

267
00:21:29,192 --> 00:21:34,279
Exercise one asks you to look at the neighbours of happy, doctor and red.

268
00:21:34,279 --> 00:21:36,091
Here's what GloVe gave me.

269
00:21:36,091 --> 00:21:38,460
Red is perfect: the other colours.

270
00:21:38,460 --> 00:21:43,478
Doctor: physician, nurse, doctors, surgeon, hospital, the medical world.

271
00:21:43,478 --> 00:21:45,220
But happy is interesting.

272
00:21:45,220 --> 00:21:49,610
Its neighbours are fragments like I'm, feel, you're and really.

273
00:21:49,610 --> 00:21:59,575
That's because happy appears in phrases like I'm really happy, so this model learned the grammatical company it keeps more than other emotions.

274
00:21:59,575 --> 00:22:03,895
That's the distributional hypothesis in action, warts and all.

275
00:22:04,745 --> 00:22:06,949
Exercise two has three analogies.

276
00:22:06,949 --> 00:22:11,355
France minus Paris plus London gives Britain, with England second.

277
00:22:11,355 --> 00:22:13,959
Good minus better plus worse gives bad.

278
00:22:13,959 --> 00:22:22,438
And dog minus puppy plus kitten gives cat, though at a cosine of only zero point six nine, with dogs and monkey not far behind.

279
00:22:22,438 --> 00:22:32,586
Try them yourself, and when they miss, ask why: is the answer a rare word, an ambiguous word, or a word used in different contexts in the training text?

280
00:22:33,435 --> 00:22:35,783
Exercise three is the apple puzzle.

281
00:22:35,783 --> 00:22:40,209
You might expect two clusters around apple, fruits and technology.

282
00:22:40,209 --> 00:22:43,026
In GloVe, technology completely dominates.

283
00:22:43,026 --> 00:22:50,202
Try it in the embedding projector, which uses a different dataset, and see whether the fruit sense appears.

284
00:22:50,202 --> 00:22:56,305
Either way, the lesson is that a static vector ends up wherever the training text pulls it.

285
00:22:57,155 --> 00:22:57,919
Part eight.

286
00:22:57,919 --> 00:22:59,587
The checkpoint exercise.

287
00:23:00,437 --> 00:23:07,613
The checkpoint asks you to build your own three dimensional embedding for ten animals, on paper or in a spreadsheet.

288
00:23:07,613 --> 00:23:12,253
Choose three properties, for example size, how domestic, and how dangerous.

289
00:23:12,253 --> 00:23:14,975
Rate each animal from minus one to plus one.

290
00:23:14,975 --> 00:23:16,089
Then compare them.

291
00:23:16,089 --> 00:23:17,698
Which animals are closest?

292
00:23:17,698 --> 00:23:24,565
Do cat and lion cluster because they're both felines, or do cat and hamster cluster because they're small pets?

293
00:23:24,565 --> 00:23:28,277
And what does that tell you about your choice of dimensions?

294
00:23:29,127 --> 00:23:35,513
I took the example ratings from the book and computed every pairwise cosine similarity.

295
00:23:35,513 --> 00:23:37,202
The answer is dramatic.

296
00:23:37,202 --> 00:23:43,221
Cat and lion have a cosine of minus zero point nine nine: they're almost opposite.

297
00:23:43,221 --> 00:23:49,828
Cat and hamster are at zero point nine seven, and cat and rabbit at zero point nine seven.

298
00:23:49,828 --> 00:23:54,673
Lion and shark are nearly identical at zero point nine nine eight.

299
00:23:54,673 --> 00:24:02,894
With these three dimensions, the space sorts animals into small, safe pets versus large, dangerous wild animals.

300
00:24:02,894 --> 00:24:05,537
Biology doesn't come into it at all.

301
00:24:06,387 --> 00:24:07,537
That's the lesson.

302
00:24:07,537 --> 00:24:11,243
The dimensions you choose decide which similarities exist.

303
00:24:11,243 --> 00:24:19,230
With size, domestic and dangerous, a goldfish is almost the same as a hamster, even though one is a fish and one is a mammal.

304
00:24:19,230 --> 00:24:24,789
Add a feline dimension, or water dwelling, or number of legs, and new groupings appear.

305
00:24:24,789 --> 00:24:27,281
Real models don't hand pick dimensions.

306
00:24:27,281 --> 00:24:33,032
They learn hundreds of them from data, so many kinds of grouping can coexist in one space.

307
00:24:33,882 --> 00:24:34,629
Part nine.

308
00:24:34,629 --> 00:24:35,525
Wrapping up.

309
00:24:36,375 --> 00:24:39,136
Here's the whole pipeline in one picture.

310
00:24:39,136 --> 00:24:45,532
A word, like king, is looked up in a big table, so its embedding is just one row of that table.

311
00:24:45,532 --> 00:24:50,111
The result is a vector, maybe seven hundred and sixty eight numbers.

312
00:24:50,111 --> 00:24:55,363
And its position in the semantic space puts it near queen and far from banana.

313
00:24:55,363 --> 00:25:01,356
In chapter five you'll build exactly this lookup table in PyTorch, with nn dot Embedding.

314
00:25:02,206 --> 00:25:03,510
The key takeaways.

315
00:25:03,510 --> 00:25:06,551
One: ASCII captures spelling, not meaning.

316
00:25:06,551 --> 00:25:11,837
Two: embeddings represent words as positions in a high dimensional space.

317
00:25:11,837 --> 00:25:17,124
Three: similar words cluster together, and unrelated words are far apart.

318
00:25:17,124 --> 00:25:23,930
Four: word arithmetic works, approximately, like king minus man plus woman landing near queen.

319
00:25:23,930 --> 00:25:27,986
Five: context teaches meaning, without any human labels.

320
00:25:27,986 --> 00:25:35,010
And six: static embeddings, especially when averaged, lose word order, so we need something more.

321
00:25:35,010 --> 00:25:37,038
That something is attention.

322
00:25:37,887 --> 00:25:42,130
A quick note on accuracy, since I'm checking the book as I go.

323
00:25:42,130 --> 00:25:53,012
I tested the chapter's examples against real vectors, and everything behaves as the book says, including its warning that analogies only work some of the time.

324
00:25:53,012 --> 00:26:02,319
A few of its listed examples don't come out first in GloVe, but the book presents them as typical relationships, not guaranteed outputs.

325
00:26:02,319 --> 00:26:10,805
The embedding numbers for king, queen and banana are illustrative, which the book itself signals by calling them simplified.

326
00:26:10,805 --> 00:26:13,679
I found no factual errors in this chapter.

327
00:26:14,529 --> 00:26:16,801
Review questions, with my answers.

328
00:26:16,801 --> 00:26:19,073
Question one: why not ASCII codes?

329
00:26:19,073 --> 00:26:27,292
Because they encode spelling, which is arbitrary, so cat and car look almost identical while cat and kitten look unrelated.

330
00:26:27,292 --> 00:26:30,900
Question two: what is an embedding, and why that name?

331
00:26:30,900 --> 00:26:35,712
It's a word's vector in a learned space where position reflects meaning.

332
00:26:35,712 --> 00:26:42,594
The name comes from maths: mapping discrete symbols into a continuous space while preserving structure.

333
00:26:42,594 --> 00:26:45,468
Question three: why hundreds of dimensions?

334
00:26:45,468 --> 00:26:55,291
Because words are similar in many different ways at the same time, royalty, gender, leadership and so on, and each kind of relationship needs room.

335
00:26:56,141 --> 00:26:59,514
Question four: explain king minus man plus woman.

336
00:26:59,514 --> 00:27:09,150
Subtracting man and adding woman moves the king vector along a consistent gender direction, and the nearest word to where you land is queen.

337
00:27:09,150 --> 00:27:13,830
It shows that embeddings encode relationships as consistent offsets.

338
00:27:13,830 --> 00:27:19,061
Question five: what does you shall know a word by the company it keeps mean?

339
00:27:19,061 --> 00:27:21,264
That meaning comes from context.

340
00:27:21,264 --> 00:27:30,143
Embedding systems learn by predicting words from their neighbours, so words used in similar contexts end up with similar vectors.

341
00:27:30,143 --> 00:27:32,965
Question six: why isn't averaging enough?

342
00:27:32,965 --> 00:27:36,131
Because it throws away order and interactions.

343
00:27:36,131 --> 00:27:40,743
Dog bites man and man bites dog average to exactly the same vector.

344
00:27:41,593 --> 00:27:43,376
Here's your practice list.

345
00:27:43,376 --> 00:27:49,824
Compute the boy, man and woman distances by hand, and the cosine of four three and three four.

346
00:27:49,824 --> 00:27:56,341
Run the gensim snippet from the notes, try ten analogies of your own, and record your hit rate.

347
00:27:56,341 --> 00:28:00,731
Explore the embedding projector and find a word with two senses.

348
00:28:00,731 --> 00:28:06,562
Do the animal checkpoint, then add a fourth dimension and watch the groupings change.

349
00:28:06,562 --> 00:28:14,656
And peek ahead at the book's companion repository on GitHub, where chapter five builds the embedding table in PyTorch.

350
00:28:14,656 --> 00:28:20,350
The code in this video is my own illustration, since Part One has no book notebook.

351
00:28:21,199 --> 00:28:22,384
That's chapter two.

352
00:28:22,384 --> 00:28:25,625
We can turn words into vectors that capture meaning.

353
00:28:25,625 --> 00:28:27,807
But meaning lives in relationships.

354
00:28:27,807 --> 00:28:34,538
In the sentence the cat sat on the mat because it was tired, how does a computer know that it means the cat?

355
00:28:34,538 --> 00:28:38,964
It has to look at the whole sentence and work out what relates to what.

356
00:28:38,964 --> 00:28:42,704
That's the problem attention solves, and it's chapter three.

357
00:28:43,553 --> 00:28:48,680
This video is based on the book Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

358
00:28:48,680 --> 00:28:57,995
Everything here is my own adaptation for study, so please support the author and buy the book at leanpub dot com slash First LLM.

359
00:28:57,995 --> 00:29:04,493
The companion code is at github dot com slash First LLM slash code, under the MIT license.

360
00:29:04,493 --> 00:29:09,042
The word vectors I used are GloVe, from the Stanford NLP group.

361
00:29:09,042 --> 00:29:12,652
Thanks for watching, and see you in chapter three.

